  "query_preview": "..\nForecast remains on track with little to no changes overall. Any\nchanges are related to the details and scenarios, which are\ncoming into better focus as much of the event is captured in the\nCAMs. Flash Flood Watch remains in effect with no planned\nchanges to the time. \n\nThe first change is a possible earlier start time to some of \nthe precip. Some CAMs are showing scattered showers with \nisolated thunderstorms by Monday morning, increasing in coverage\nthrough the day. The second change is an upward trend in the \nforecast rainfall. While widespread 1-2\" of rain is likely area\nwide (exception of Western Pima County 0.25-0.75\"), there is an\nincreasing signal of 2-4\" for the Sky Islands and the White \nMtns. In fact, some 75th/90th percentile REFS/RRFS guidance \nsuggest 4\"+ is possible in the White Mtns, possibly extending SW\ninto the Sky Islands. Given the duration, possible training, \nand threat for thunderstorms, this seems reasonable. Another \nthing of note is the EFFI and Shift of Tales, which supports the\npotential for these higher end totals.\n\nNow zooming into the Tucson Metro, generally 0.5-1.5\" is \nexpected for the event. The 75th/95th percentile (reasonable \nworst case) is pointing to totals of 2-3\"+. While this is a \ndistinct possibility, it will favor upslope regions near the \nCatalina/Rincon Mtns and/or require repeated rounds (training) \nof heavier showers/storms. Some of the CAMs are showing \nvariations of this possibility and it bears watching. Another \nthing to consider is this will not be your classic Monsoonal \nthunderstorms. It will be deep tropical moisture with widespread\nshowers and isolated to scattered thunderstorms, which may\npersist off and on for a longer duration Monday into Tuesday. \nWith every event, there will be areas that under perform and \nover perform, dependent on where these heavier bands of rainfall\nsetup. We will make adjustments and updates to better depict \nthese scenarios as we move into Monday and confidence increases.\nHowever, we want to reiterate there will be significant \nrainfall for portions of SEAZ and at least isolated to perhaps\nscattered instances of Flash Flooding. WPC maintains a Slight \nRisk over much of the area for Monday and has a Moderate Risk \njust east of Tucson to the NM border on Tuesday. Moderate Risk \nExcessive Rainfall Outlooks for the Desert Southwest from WPC \nare fairly rare.\n\nAs the upper level low moves across Arizona into Tuesday, the \nthreat for flooding and heavy rain persist into Tuesday. Given \nthe forcing, marginal CAPE, and abundant shear, strong to \nperhaps an isolated severe storm will be possible. Even brief \ntransient low top supercells will be possible. \n\nActivity shifts east by Wednesday, with the possibility for\nlingering flooding on area washes. Easterly gusty winds may be\npossible by Thursday and Friday, but confidence is low at this\ntime.",
